he emergency room  yeah and it just went on and on with  people saying they didn't want to be  held there against their will and you  know just absolute in total chaos listen  [Music]  yeah so anyway that was what was going  on well the surgical resident was trying  to explain the procedure to me and at  one point I think she said you know the  anesthesiologist is going to come talk  to you soon and I think I vaguely  remember saying that's not the  anesthesiologist is it and it was a  light-hearted moment in the middle of  what was otherwise not not a fun day  so things I mean it was like all night  eventually you know the anesthesiologist  showed up we talked about the plan  I met the surgeon and here's an  interesting story by the way about the  surgeon the surgeon comes in and I find  out that the surgeons specialty doctor  matinees who did a great job he  specializes in endocrine surgery and I'm  thinking back you know not that long ago  I read the checklist manifesto by dr.  Atul Gawande  here and who's a great doctor here in  Boston who also specializes in I believe  it's endocrine cancer surgery so I said  to my doctor do you know Atul Gawande  and he said yeah we share an office  actually and one of the last things I  remember when I was sedated already with  very strong valium which is great great  great stuff if you're anxious I've high  medical anxiety mmm this stuff works I  remember being in the o.r and everybody  going through in a way that was  reminiscent of what Atul Gawande sort of  developed and writes about in the  checklist manifesto going through what  is each person on the o.r teams what is  the role of each person in the room who  is the patient what are they doing I  remember actually saying the last thing  I remember is it seems like everybody's  read the checklist manifesto and I  remember people in the room laughing and  then waking up in the recovery room  and it was like an hour procedure it  went as well as these things I think can  g